WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.458
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of "Cloud Performance Atlas,"

00:00:02.458 --> 00:00:04.860
I help some cryptocurrency
fans with their Datastore

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:05.880
transaction.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:08.370
Is it the right time to invest
in the performance market?

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:09.707
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:09.707 --> 00:00:12.690
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:16.180
Like many start-ups
today, Coin All the Things

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:18.480
is riding the
cryptocurrency wave.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:21.120
And in order to provide
real-time dashboards,

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:24.060
they mirror all validated
blockchain transactions

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.130
back to Datastore
so that customers

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:27.870
can look at historical records.

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:30.090
Sadly though, they
quickly ran into an issue

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:31.710
with their read-write
performance,

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:34.740
being limited to only about
one entity update per second,

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:36.282
far slower than
what they needed.

00:00:36.282 --> 00:00:37.740
Thankfully, I was
able to reach out

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:40.590
to some fellow advocates,
who were able to help track

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:41.684
down the problems.

00:00:41.684 --> 00:00:43.350
After a few emails
and a couple of hours

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:44.900
of looking at their
tech stack, we

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:46.860
were able to pinpoint
a critical optimization

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:50.630
for their Datastore usage,
transactions and batching.

00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:53.040
See, in a hurry to get
their code up and running,

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:56.280
Coin All the Things did
a lot of copy-pasting

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:57.840
from sample references,
and ended up

00:00:57.840 --> 00:00:59.640
in a place where
each of their calls

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.830
executed as a single action.

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:04.860
And since each request has its
own transactional overhead,

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:06.510
this quickly became
the bottleneck

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:08.710
during high
performance workloads.

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:12.060
The solution, of course, was
to batch these sets of calls

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:14.340
together into a
single HTTP request

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:16.950
so that the overhead is
lowered across the entire set

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:17.730
of updates.

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:20.910
Now, there's two primary ways
to do batching in Datastore.

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:23.730
The first is transactions,
which allow a set of operations

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:24.600
to be atomic.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:28.050
That is, either the entire
set succeeds or it fails.

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:30.870
There's no chance of half of
the update to have occurred,

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:33.540
which is really helpful when
wanting to enforce consistency

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:35.010
to your observers.

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:36.690
Sadly though,
transactions are only

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:39.690
limited to a set of 25
operations at a time.

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:41.850
This is where the
batching APIs come in.

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:44.040
They don't have the
same size restrictions

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:46.080
and they don't do
updates atomically,

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.420
so observers should take
eventual consistency

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:50.550
into account when interacting
with the database.

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:53.190
Now, head to head, we can
see that both transactions

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:55.980
and batching outperform
their linear counterparts.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:58.410
But to be clear here,
each of these tools

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:01.320
are valuable for a
specific use case,

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:03.060
and shouldn't be
adopted universally

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:04.860
without understanding
the trade-offs.

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:08.310
Now, for Coin All the
Things, the choice was clear.

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:10.500
They split some of their
operations between batching

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.780
and transactions, depending
on the consistency model

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:15.750
they needed, and saw
performance skyrocket, along

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:17.494
with their ICO value.

00:02:17.494 --> 00:02:19.410
If you'd like to know
more about these topics,

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:21.670
check out the article listed
in the description below.

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:23.370
And don't forget to check
out the rest of the "Cloud

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:25.140
Performance Atlas"
content, because when

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:27.900
it comes to performance,
every millisecond counts.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:30.950
[MUSIC PLAYING]

